digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56815-9
  rankdir="TB";
  "B3" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given that multifunctional-dielectric exploration aligns with in-sensor computing needs and that transferable solution-processed oxides are a promising underexplored platform, therefore a best explanation/design direction is a transferable, wet-chemistry oxide dielectric that is also multifunctional."];
  "C1" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given that high-performance 2D FETs/memory and in-sensor/memory computing are desired and that such devices require a high-quality high-κ dielectric with a clean 2D interface, therefore high-quality high-κ dielectric integration is a key requirement in 2D electronics."];
  "C2" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given that direct ALD/deposition on 2D surfaces causes poor nucleation and defective/doped interfaces and that problematic deposition implies alternative integration routes are needed, therefore alternatives to direct ALD high-κ integration are needed for 2D semiconductors."];
  "C3" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given that nanogap/vdW interfaces suppress leakage and interfacial interaction and that transferred oxides can form such nanogaps, therefore transferable oxides with nanogap interfaces are promising high-quality dielectrics for 2D devices."];
  "C5" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given that gas-to-solid dielectrics are limited while wet-chemistry oxides are diverse/scalable and that such diversity suggests exploration as transferable vdW dielectrics, therefore solution-processed oxides are a promising yet underexplored dielectric platform for 2D electronics."];
  "C7" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given the need for alternatives to direct ALD for high-quality dielectrics on 2D materials and the promise of wet-chemistry-derived transferable oxides, therefore a plausible hypothesis is to develop a wet-chemistry-derived, transferable oxide dielectric that can be vdW-integrated (and potentially multifunctional) in 2D devices."];
  "I2" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given that integrating high-quality high-κ dielectrics on vdW 2D surfaces is a critical challenge and that ALD typically needs buffer/seeding layers on 2D surfaces, therefore direct-deposition routes are constrained and motivate alternative dielectric integration."];
  "I4" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given that engineering the dielectric to be multifunctional is worth exploring and that in-sensor computing benefits from multifunctional layers that reduce complexity, therefore multifunctional-dielectric exploration is aligned with all-in-one heterostructure design needs."];
  "I5" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given that gas-to-solid dielectrics are limited in types/functions and that solution-processed oxides as 2D dielectrics are unexplored but could expand candidates if transferable/vdW-integrable, therefore exploring transferable solution-processed oxides is well-motivated."];
  "I6" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given that CCTO/TMD devices show near-ideal SS and ultra-small hysteresis consistent with a high-quality oxide–2D interface and that nanogap/vdW-integrated transferred oxides can suppress leakage and interaction, therefore the observed device metrics are consistent with nanogap-enabled high-quality dielectric integration."];
  "I8" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given that CaF2 gas-to-solid epitaxial dielectrics can be excellent (e.g., back-gate) and that traditional gas-to-solid dielectric options are limited in material types/functions for 2D devices, therefore even when a gas-to-solid dielectric works, the overall material/function space remains constrained, motivating broader platforms."];
  "K1" [label="(0,0,0) If high-performance 2D FETs/memory and in-sensor/memory computing are desired, then integrating a high-quality high-κ dielectric with a clean 2D interface is necessary."];
  "K2" [label="(0,0,0) If direct deposition of high-κ dielectrics on 2D materials yields poor nucleation and defective/doped interfaces, then alternative dielectric integration routes (e.g., transfer/vdW integration or different dielectric chemistries) are needed."];
  "K3" [label="(0,0,0) If a transferred oxide forms a nanogap/vdW interface to a 2D semiconductor, then gate leakage and interfacial chemical interaction can be suppressed, enabling good dielectric behavior even for some moderate-bandgap oxides."];
  "K4" [label="(0,0,0) If in-sensor computing heterostructures benefit from multifunctional layers, then making the dielectric layer multifunctional (e.g., dielectric + optical sensing/programmability) is a plausible device-design strategy."];
  "K5" [label="(0,0,0) If gas-to-solid dielectric options are limited but wet-chemistry oxides are diverse and scalable, then exploring wet-chemistry-derived oxides as transferable vdW dielectrics could expand the dielectric/material design space for 2D electronics."];
  "N1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently, 2D semiconductors (e.g., TMDs) have potential to ease silicon IC challenges (scaling size, heat, memory walls)."];
  "N10" [label="(10,0,0) Currently, a nanogap between TMDs and transferred oxides suppresses gate leakage and reduces physical contact, allowing even moderate-bandgap oxides to work as good dielectrics (counterintuitive)."];
  "N11" [label="(11,0,0) Currently, in all-in-one in-sensor computing heterostructures, one expects a sensing (stimuli-responsive) layer and ideally a multifunctional layer that transfers signals and reduces layer count/complexity."];
  "N12" [label="(12,0,0) Currently, it is worth exploring engineering the dielectric layer to be multifunctional (beyond only dielectric)."];
  "N13" [label="(13,0,0) Currently, traditional gas-to-solid dielectrics for 2D devices are limited in material types and functions due to precursor restrictions or transfer prerequisites."];
  "N14" [label="(14,0,0) Currently, wet-chemistry (liquid-to-solid) metal oxides are low-cost, scalable, and versatile in category/function, widely used in thin-film electronics (displays/sensors) and energy devices."];
  "N15" [label="(15,0,0) Currently, using solution-processed metal oxides as dielectrics in 2D devices is unexplored, but could expand candidates for advanced 2D electronics if the oxides are transferable and vdW-integrable."];
  "N16" [label="(16,0,0) Currently, a wet-chemistry-derived method can prepare amorphous CCTO (a perovskite-type complex oxide) that is transferable and incorporable into 2D devices."];
  "N17" [label="(17,0,0) Currently, CCTO/TMD devices show SS ≈ 67 mV/dec and ultra-small hysteresis, which corroborates a high-quality oxide–2D interface aided by dielectric properties and a nanogap."];
  "N18" [label="(18,0,0) Currently, using CCTO optical properties enables optically writing and electrically erasing a floating gate in a simple FET, leading to reconfigurable logic-in-sensor operation."];
  "N2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently, 2D semiconductors’ atomic thickness enables strong electrostatic control and their vdW/dangling-bond-free surfaces enable Lego-type integration, which can boost FET and memory performance."];
  "N3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently, 2D devices have enabled all-in-one perception–memory–computation (beyond von Neumann), enabling in-sensor/memory computing for pattern recognition/processing."];
  "N4" [label="(4,0,0) Currently, the vdW (dangling-bond-free) surface of 2D semiconductors creates a critical challenge: integrating high-quality high-κ dielectrics like silicon did."];
  "N5" [label="(5,0,0) Currently, direct ALD of conventional amorphous high-κ dielectrics (HfO2, Al2O3) on 2D surfaces nucleates poorly due to no dangling bonds, causing defective interfaces and undesirable doping."];
  "N6" [label="(6,0,0) Currently, a buffer/seeding layer is typically required before ALD on 2D surfaces."];
  "N7" [label="(7,0,0) Currently, gas-to-solid epitaxial CaF2 can serve as an excellent dielectric, at least for back-gate configurations."];
  "N8" [label="(8,0,0) Currently, dielectric transfer techniques can separate dielectric fabrication from integration (looser material/temperature constraints) but require dielectrics to be transferable."];
  "N9" [label="(9,0,0) Currently, SrTiO3 (high permittivity) can be fabricated on sacrificial layers and transferred as a freestanding flake into TMD devices."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper proposes to develop and evaluate wet-chemistry-derived transferable amorphous CCTO films for van der Waals integration with 2D TMD semiconductors, testing whether they can serve simultaneously as high-κ dielectrics and photo-responsive layers for low-hysteresis, near-ideal-subthreshold FETs and optically written, electrically erased logic-in-sensor operation.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A material platform that addresses high-quality dielectric integration, avoids problematic direct deposition, is transferable/vdW-integrable, and adds optical programmability is a coherent research direction for advanced 2D electronics and logic-in-sensor devices."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given that dielectric transfer separates dielectric fabrication from integration while requiring transferable dielectrics, and that transferred oxides forming vdW/nanogap interfaces are plausible high-quality gating candidates, dielectric transfer is a promising practical route toward vdW-integrated dielectrics."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given that CCTO optical properties enable optically written and electrically erased floating-gate behavior leading to reconfigurable logic-in-sensor operation, and that multifunctional dielectrics are a plausible strategy for in-sensor heterostructures, CCTO-like multifunctional dielectric behavior supports exploring simplified reconfigurable logic-in-sensor 2D devices."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given the broader motivation to pursue high-performance 2D electronic device stacks and the requirement for high-quality high-κ dielectric integration with clean 2D interfaces, dielectric integration is a central bottleneck and opportunity in 2D device research."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given constrained direct-deposition routes for high-κ dielectrics on vdW 2D surfaces and the repaired support that dielectric transfer is a promising practical route, alternative integration strategies should emphasize transfer/vdW approaches and transferable dielectrics."];
  "R20" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given that alternative dielectric integration should emphasize transfer/vdW approaches and that transferable solution-processed oxides align with multifunctional-dielectric exploration, a wet-chemistry-derived transferable multifunctional oxide is a strengthened design hypothesis for 2D device needs."];
  "R21" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given the strengthened design rationale for wet-chemistry-derived transferable multifunctional oxides and the hypothesis of developing wet-chemistry-derived transferable oxide dielectrics, a wet-chemistry-derived transferable perovskite-like oxide such as CCTO is a plausible candidate for combining dielectric gating with additional device functionality."];
  "R22" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given CCTO/TMD device metrics consistent with nanogap-enabled high-quality interfaces and repaired support for CCTO-like multifunctional dielectric behavior in optically programmable logic-in-sensor operation, CCTO-like wet-chemistry transferable oxides are credible candidates for reconfigurable logic-in-sensor 2D device platforms."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given that in-sensor computing heterostructures benefit from multifunctional layers, making the dielectric layer multifunctional is a plausible device-design strategy for reducing layer count and supporting sensing or programmability functions."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given reported transferable SrTiO3 integration with TMD devices and observations that nanogaps between TMDs and transferred oxides suppress leakage and physical contact, transferred oxides that form vdW/nanogap interfaces are supported as plausible candidates for high-quality gating in 2D devices, without implying that all transferable oxides will work."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given the hypothesis of developing wet-chemistry-derived transferable oxide dielectrics for vdW-integrated 2D devices and the demonstrated ability to prepare transferable amorphous CCTO by wet chemistry, amorphous CCTO is a concrete candidate material for testing that dielectric-integration hypothesis."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given that 2D semiconductors may address silicon IC scaling, heat, and memory-wall challenges and that their atomic thickness and vdW surfaces can boost FET and memory performance, 2D semiconductors provide strong motivation for pursuing high-performance 2D electronic device stacks."];
  "K1" -> "C1" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "N3" -> "C1" [label="deduction-case"];
